===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# {{lb|en|ecology}} Of, or relating to [[symbiosis]]; [[living]] [[together]].
#: {{ux|en|A lichen is a fungus with '''symbiotic''' algae among its cells.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Quite interesting: A quietly intriguing column from the brains behind QI, the BBC quiz show. This week; QI orchids you not|work={{w|The Daily Telegraph}} (Weekend)|date=5 April 2014|page=W22|passage=Orchids rely on fungi to reproduce. Their tiny seeds don't have any on-board nutrients (like beans and apples) and will not germinate until they are infected by a '''symbiotic''' fungus which supplies them with food. Known as a protocorm, this tiny orchid-fungus ball grows, turns green and eventually starts to photosynthesise.}}
# Of a relationship with [[mutual]] [[benefit]] between two [[individual]]s, [[organism]]s, or [[group]]s.
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=The Christian Science Monitor|date=2025-01-30|author=Dina Kraft|title=How Gaza hostage-release drama works for both Hamas and Netanyahu|titleurl=https://www.csmonitor.com/World/Middle-East/2025/0130/gaza-israel-hostages-hamas-netanyahu|passage=[Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu's] arguably '''symbiotic''' relationship with Hamas goes back to his election in 1996, when he upset left-wing incumbent Shimon Peres following a spate of Hamas bombings, which Mr. Netanyahu said proved the peace process could not be trusted.}}

====Usage notes====
Although the biologic meaning of ''symbiotic'' strictly refers to "living together", regardless of the nature of the relationship, in casual speech the word typically implies a beneficial relationship.

